# Olympus — FactoryOps — environment configuration template
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values. Nothing generates it for you — setup.sh
# does not create or edit `.env`.
# Never commit `.env`. Production secrets come from Cerulean Vault (SecretOps).

# --- General ----------------------------------------------------------------
APP_NAME=olympus
# Public hosts (DNS + TLS provisioned by Cerulean / NPM Edge)
BASE_DOMAIN=olympus.innotel.us
DOMAIN=olympus.innotel.us
# (OLYMPUS_PORT is retired. The `olympus` container publishes no host port — it
#  has no network surface anything talks to, and the port it used to claim (20128)
#  belongs to the gateway. The gateway's own port is OMNIROUTE_PORT below.)

# --- OmniRoute (model gateway — OpenAI-compatible, single provider pool) ----
# ONE gateway for the whole platform, and this stack does not run it. It is the
# Group 2 service (`2-voice/`, mesh `10.10.2.1`, Consul service `omniroute`),
# which is what makes one provider pool and one place to rotate possible. The
# default below is that gateway's `/v1` door on the mesh: the SSO proxy on the
# gateway's host, where /v1 is exempted from the Authentik session while the
# dashboard behind it still requires one.
#
# Only one topology changes this value:
#   on this host  the gateway is published on THIS host's loopback (the
#                 deployment where Olympus and OmniRoute share a box). Then use
#                 http://127.0.0.1:20128/v1 and run every service that talks to
#                 it with compose.host-gateway.yml — the host-side build runner
#                 and the CLI scripts read this same file, and a service that
#                 cannot see the host's loopback (Studio, on the bridge
#                 network) silently resolves 127.0.0.1 to itself.
#                 `make docker-studio-up` is how Studio gets there.
#
# The gateway knows no other door: it is the process holding every provider
# credential and exactly one of it exists.
#
# THE PUBLIC NAME IS NOT AN OPTION, and the API key is not why. /v1 is not
# key-authenticated on this gateway — no key, a bogus key and the real one all answer
# 200, and an unauthenticated POST returns a completion (requireLogin=false means the
# gateway has no gate of its own; see docs/gateway-sso.md). The SSO proxy exempts /v1
# so that API clients are possible at all, which is fine on the LAN and is exactly why
# https://gateway.olympus.innotel.us/v1/* is refused with 403 by edge rule
# (`make gateway-edge --deny-path /v1`, checked by `make gateway-edge-check`). Send the
# key if you like; what gates the LAN door is being on the LAN.
#
# MESH_GATEWAY_HOST is the gateway server's mesh address; set it (or the whole
# URL) when the mesh is not the 10.10.2.1 default.
#
# A host that sees neither this box nor the mesh dials the gateway host's LAN
# address instead — http://<omniroute-host-LAN-IP>:20128/v1 — and that host must
# opt its OmniRoute into a LAN bind (OMNIROUTE_LAN_BIND in capstone's .env).
# Measured from a moved Olympus: 10.10.2.1:20128 refuses, the LAN address answers.
MESH_GATEWAY_HOST=10.10.2.1
# Codex is the primary coding brain, over the Responses API (wire_api=responses).
OMNIROUTE_BASE_URL=http://10.10.2.1:20128/v1
OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=change-me-omniroute-api-key
# The build model, and the setting most worth getting right. It must be a model that
# CALLS TOOLS. One that answers in prose still "completes" while writing nothing: the
# app directory stays empty, the agent looks busy for minutes, and there is no error to
# read.
#
# Check a candidate in two steps, because the cheap one is not enough. Measured on
# this deployment, four models passed the two-turn gateway probe and then answered
# `429` to Codex's first real request:
#
#   1. the probe     make build-model-check ARGS="--model <provider>/<model>"
#   2. the real one  the `codex exec` invocation in docs/build-model.md
#
# Neither the combo nor a bare free model is the answer. `auto/coding` walks the whole
# catalogue (1397 routing decisions for one request) and then pins the turn to whichever
# member answered, which is how a build lands on a provider that refuses Codex's tool
# calls. Of the ten provider accounts this gateway holds, nine cannot serve a build at
# all — out of credit, not eligible, or answering a tool shape Codex cannot use — so the
# chain below is: the model MEASURED to finish a build, then the better model for when
# the first writes nothing.
#
#   oc/mimo-v2.5-free               built builds/todo-list in 145s, first attempt
#   gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview   better, but the free tier locks a model out for
#                                   1800s after a burst
#
# docs/build-model.md has the whole sweep (including every refusal, so nobody
# re-measures them) and the options for real capacity. A provider with real credits is
# still the only durable fix.
OMNIROUTE_MODEL=oc/mimo-v2.5-free
# Tried when the first entry wrote nothing. Set it equal to OMNIROUTE_MODEL to give up
# after the first model rather than let the gateway search.
OMNIROUTE_MODEL_FALLBACK=gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview
# Only `scripts/omniroute-restore-providers.py` reads this: restoring the gateway's
# provider connections when they are missing needs to authenticate to it, and a stock
# OmniRoute container starts with the dashboard password `CHANGEME`. Set it here (or
# pass --password) and CHANGE THE GATEWAY PASSWORD — a gateway that holds provider
# credentials on a published port should not keep the default.
# OMNIROUTE_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# --- Studio — vibe-coding web UI (web/studio) -------------------------------
# Next.js app: describe an app in plain language and it builds one. Studio reads
# the OMNIROUTE_* values above server-side only — the gateway key is never sent
# to, or bundled for, the browser.
#   make studio-dev     # http://localhost:$(STUDIO_PORT)
STUDIO_PORT=3001
# The address the NPM proxy hosts forward to — this stack's own LAN address, not
# the docker0 gateway. The compose default (172.17.0.1) works only while the
# edge runs on the same host: from a separate edge host, 172.17.0.1 is the
# *edge's* docker0, where nothing is listening. Set it whenever this box and
# Cerulean are not the same machine.
STUDIO_EDGE_HOST=
# Public host Cerulean points at the edge (DNS/TLS via Cerulean + NPM Edge).
# Read by `make studio-oidc`, which registers this host's callback
# (https://<host>/api/auth/callback) as a redirect URI on Studio's Authentik
# application. Studio itself does not read it: at runtime the callback is
# derived from the edge's x-forwarded-host/-proto, so every name Studio answers
# on has to be registered once (add extras with `make studio-oidc ARGS="--redirect-uri ..."`).
STUDIO_PUBLIC_HOST=studio.olympus.innotel.us
# Where Studio's "Build it" writes a request for the host-side build runner, and
# where it reads that build's status back. Studio cannot run `make app` itself
# (its image carries no toolchain), so the queue is the entire interface. Under
# compose this is the bind mount of the repo's .factory/build-queue.
STUDIO_BUILD_QUEUE_DIR=/app/build-queue
# Optional shared gate for /api/generate. Empty = open on a trusted network or
# behind an identity-aware proxy (Authentik OIDC).
STUDIO_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# Where saved apps are stored. Per identity — keyed on the control-plane user id
# when tenancy is configured, on the OIDC subject otherwise (both hashed to a
# directory name, and an existing subject-keyed library is adopted in place, so
# turning tenancy on does not orphan it). One JSON file per app. The compose
# `studio` service points this at a named volume so the library survives a
# rebuild; leave it empty for a local run and it defaults to ./data/studio beside
# the checkout (gitignored).
STUDIO_DATA_DIR=
# --- Studio tenancy (Distro's control plane — convergence §5.2) -------------
# With these set, a turn is spent on the signed-in user's OWN gateway key: Studio
# resolves the Authentik subject to a control-plane account, mints that user's
# key on first sight, checks the quota before dispatch and records usage after.
# The same CONTROL_INTERNAL_TOKEN must be set on the control plane (Distro's
# `.env`, generated by its bootstrap).
#
# Empty = single-operator: Studio spends the shared OMNIROUTE_API_KEY above, with
# no per-user attribution — which is what it did before tenancy existed. The key
# is strict once configured: no quiet fallback to the shared key, because that
# would move one user's spend onto the operator's. The quota check, by contrast,
# fails open (the gateway key's own hard cap is the backstop).
# The control plane is Distro's (Group 5). Point this at the address it publishes
# 20140 on: `http://distro-control-plane:20140` if Studio is in that compose
# network, else the host/mesh address (e.g. http://10.10.5.1:20140).
CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL=
CONTROL_INTERNAL_TOKEN=
# Where "Export to factory" writes build-request specs. Leave empty to use
# build-requests/ at the repository root — under compose that is the bind mount
# in docker-compose.yml (/app/build-requests), and a local `next dev` run
# resolves to the checkout itself. Set it to keep exports somewhere else.
STUDIO_FACTORY_REQUESTS_DIR=
# Where packaged website builds live. `scripts/package-website.py` writes
# builds/<slug>/dist/ and builds/<slug>/site.zip; with this mounted (read-only,
# as the compose `studio` service does) Studio's download button serves that exact
# archive. Without it Studio zips the saved source itself, which is the same files
# minus the built dist/.
STUDIO_BUILDS_DIR=/app/builds
# Generations per minute per signed-in identity on /api/generate (default 20,
# hard cap 600). Protects the shared model pool from a runaway retry loop.
# Set 0 (or off) to disable the limit entirely — sensible for a single-operator
# deployment behind the IdP; a typo falls back to the default, never to unlimited.
# This is the deployment default: Studio's Settings panel can override it at
# runtime (stored beside the saved apps, under STUDIO_DATA_DIR), and clearing
# that override from the UI returns to this value.
STUDIO_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=
# Override only if the gateway exposes the chat completions route elsewhere.
# OMNIROUTE_CHAT_PATH=/chat/completions

# --- Convex — the other data target a plan may choose ------------------------
# Convex is a realtime database and function runtime, and Atlas runs one. It is not a
# second builder: when a request genuinely needs live, shared state the planner may
# answer `convex` as the project's data *target*, and a Convex-targeted project is
# still built, packaged and run as a container — the container hosts the client, and
# Convex holds the schema and the functions.
#
# The deployment's address. The browser has to reach it, so it is public by design:
# `scripts/package-project.py` writes it into the image under the three names clients
# look for (CONVEX_URL, VITE_CONVEX_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL). A Convex-targeted
# plan without it is refused rather than packaged, because the alternative is a
# container serving a client whose every read comes back blank.
# Atlas's own .env.example carries the backend's ports (CONVEX_BACKEND_PORT 3210,
# CONVEX_SITE_PORT 3211) and CONVEX_HOST — set this to the name the edge serves.
CONVEX_URL=
# Only a plan whose own build step deploys the functions needs this, and only a key
# scoped to THIS deployment may be used. Packaging passes it as a docker
# `--build-arg` and never as an `ENV`, so it does not reach the published image — but
# Docker does record build arguments in the image metadata, which is why the
# backend's platform admin key (Atlas `CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_ADMIN_KEY`) is never read
# here. Mint a deployment key instead (`npx convex deploy-key create`).
CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY=

# --- Telegram — interactive agent interface (Hermes 3 + Codex via OmniRoute) --
# Create a bot with @BotFather on Telegram: /newbot -> paste the HTTP API token.
# Stored in `.factory-env` as TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN by setup.sh (not here), but
# listed for completeness — never commit the real token.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=change-me-telegram-bot-token
# Optional — alert delivery for the daily credential check
# (scripts/studio-token-alert.sh, scheduled by scripts/install-token-check-timer.sh).
# With both set (and real), the check messages this chat when the Studio
# registration credential is lapsing or unusable; with either empty it stays
# journal-only. Find the chat id by messaging the bot and reading
# https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates. Real values here also feed the
# factory's Telegram interface when it reads this file.
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# --- Archon — workflow engine configuration ---------------------------------
# Mirrors `.archon/config.yaml` (Telegram provider + assistants.codex model).
ARCHON_BINARY=./core-modules/archon/bin/archon
ARCHON_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:20128/v1
ARCHON_DEFAULT_MODEL=auto/coding
TELEGRAM_PROVIDER=openrouter
TELEGRAM_MODEL=nousresearch/hermes-3-llama-3-70b:free

# --- Authentik (OIDC identity provider — Cerulean's Authentik) --------------
# Studio is its own application on the shared Cerulean Authentik: the issuer
# path IS the application slug, so this is application `studio`. Create or
# repair it with `make studio-oidc` (scripts/authentik-studio-app.py), which
# needs AUTHENTIK_* below; `make studio-oidc-check` then confirms the issuer
# answers discovery.
OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://auth.cerulean.innotel.us/application/o/studio/
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=studio
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me-oidc-client-secret
# Authentik API — used only by the registration script, never by Studio.
#
# This is deliberately NOT an administrator token. On the Cerulean platform it is
# a dated service account (`olympus-studio`) in a role
# (`olympus-studio-registration`) that may read flows, scope mappings and signing
# keys and create or update an OAuth2 provider + application — and nothing else
# (no users, groups, roles, outposts, no deletes).
#
# The value lives in Cerulean Vault, so the reference is what belongs here. The
# script resolves it through Cerulean Vault; a literal token still works, so a
# checkout with no Vault is unaffected. Empty = `make studio-oidc` has nothing to
# authenticate with; Studio never reads either of these at runtime.
#
# Rebuild it (and re-date it) with `make studio-token-rotate
# AUTHENTIK_HOST=<host running cerulean-authentik>`, which mints it in the
# Authentik shell and writes it to Vault; `make studio-token-check` reports its
# remaining life and exits non-zero once it is inside its warning window. It is
# minted in the shell, not the REST API, because Authentik 2026.8 forces
# api-intent tokens to the tenant's default expiry (minutes=30 here) whatever
# you ask for.
AUTHENTIK_URL=https://auth.cerulean.innotel.us
AUTHENTIK_TOKEN='vault://cerulean/olympus/authentik#AUTHENTIK_TOKEN'
# Empty = Studio derives the callback per request from the edge's forwarded
# host/proto, so one deployment works under every public name it is served on
# (e.g. olympus.innotel.us and studio.olympus.innotel.us) — provided each host is
# registered as a redirect URI on the application (`make studio-oidc` registers
# STUDIO_PUBLIC_HOST and BASE_DOMAIN for you). Pinning a single host here makes
# any other one unusable: an unregistered redirect_uri is rejected at /authorize.
OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=
# Studio (web/studio) uses these directly: leave OIDC_ISSUER_URL empty (or the
# client secret as the change-me placeholder) and Studio runs with auth disabled.
# Quoted on purpose: unquoted, `VAR=a b c` assigns to the *command's* environment
# and leaves the shell variable empty, so a sourced .env silently drops it.
OIDC_SCOPES="openid email profile"
# Group allow-list for Studio — comma-separated, matched exactly against the
# token's `groups` claim. Names are case-sensitive, and names containing spaces
# are fine (e.g. OIDC_ALLOWED_GROUPS=Cerulean,authentik Agent-Users).
#   empty  = any authenticated user may use Studio (default)
#   set    = only members of a listed group may, and everyone else gets 403
# The Cerulean platform grants access through the `cerulean-platform` group, so
# that is the value to set when you want the group gate on rather than "any
# account in the tenant may spend the gateway".
# Authentik releases `groups` through the `profile` scope, which is in
# OIDC_SCOPES above — so this works without changing the provider.
# Leaving it set with no matching group locks everyone out, including you.
OIDC_ALLOWED_GROUPS=
# Signs the Studio session + OIDC flow cookies. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Empty = derived from OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET (fine for a single host).
STUDIO_SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Gateway dashboard SSO (the OmniRoute dashboard) ------------------------
# The model gateway's dashboard manages provider credentials, so it should not be
# reachable on a shared password. It is put behind Cerulean Authentik by an
# identity-aware proxy (oauth2-proxy) rather than by OmniRoute's own OIDC, whose
# callback cannot validate an Authentik ID token — it strips the trailing slash
# that Authentik always puts in `iss` and then demands an exact match.
# docs/gateway-sso.md carries the evidence.
#
# Setup, in order:
#   1. make gateway-oidc ARGS=--rotate-secret   # registers the client, prints the secret
#   2. put that secret in GATEWAY_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET below, and set the host
#   3. make gateway-sso-up && make gateway-sso-check
GATEWAY_PUBLIC_HOST=gateway.olympus.innotel.us
GATEWAY_SLUG=omniroute
# The issuer keeps its trailing slash: that is what Authentik publishes, and the
# proxy compares it exactly. The issuer path IS the application slug.
GATEWAY_OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://auth.cerulean.innotel.us/application/o/omniroute/
GATEWAY_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=omniroute
# Printed once by `make gateway-oidc ARGS=--rotate-secret`; Authentik stores it
# write-only, so it cannot be read back out of the provider.
GATEWAY_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Signs the proxy's session cookie. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
GATEWAY_SSO_COOKIE_SECRET=
# The real door. Any verified email clears the OIDC step; only members of this
# group get in, which is something OmniRoute's own allow-list cannot express —
# it takes subjects and emails, so "anyone in cerulean-platform" is not a
# sentence it can say. Same group Studio already uses.
GATEWAY_SSO_ALLOWED_GROUP=cerulean-platform
# Where the proxy listens for the edge, and what it forwards to. The upstream is
# the gateway's loopback binding; see compose.gateway-sso.yml for why the proxy
# has to share the host's network namespace to reach it.
GATEWAY_SSO_PORT=20129
GATEWAY_SSO_UPSTREAM=http://127.0.0.1:20128
# The session store, and why it is not a cookie. A cookie session carries the
# email, the ID token and every group the identity claims; an account in a few
# dozen Authentik groups overflows the 4KB cookie limit, oauth2-proxy splits the
# session across several cookies, and the resulting `Set-Cookie` headers are
# larger than the edge's proxy buffer — which turns the request that would have
# finished the login into a 502 from the edge. Server-side sessions have no such
# ceiling. Bound to loopback; losing the store costs a re-login, nothing else.
#
# Hex and not base64, because this value is interpolated into a
# `redis://:PASSWORD@host/0` URL: a '+' or '/' from base64 would be parsed as
# part of the address rather than as part of the password. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
GATEWAY_SSO_REDIS_PASSWORD=
GATEWAY_SSO_REDIS_PORT=16379
# The LAN address of THIS host, which `make gateway-edge` points the NPM proxy
# host at (the edge is on another machine and reaches the proxy, never the
# gateway). Not the gateway's address, not the edge's.
GATEWAY_SSO_EDGE_FORWARD_HOST=
# Only for OmniRoute's OWN OIDC, which is inert today (see the file header above).
# Emails (or hashed subjects) allowed to sign in, comma-separated. OmniRoute
# rejects an enabled OIDC client with an empty list, and it is email-based only.
GATEWAY_OIDC_ALLOWED_SUBJECTS=

# --- Published websites (Studio → static host) ------------------------------
# Studio builds two kinds of thing. A *website* is Vite + React and is finished
# only once `scripts/package-website.py` has built it, so it needs somewhere to be
# served from and a name to answer on.
#
# ONE WILDCARD, THEN INSTANT PUBLISHES. `make sites-wildcard` runs once: it creates
# `*.studio.olympus.innotel.us` and a single certificate covering every name under
# it. After that `make site-publish SLUG=<slug>` adds only a proxy host at the NPM
# edge — seconds, with no DNS change and no certificate issuance. Before the
# wildcard, each publish meant its own record and its own Let's Encrypt order.
#
# `make sites-up` starts the static file server for the staged tree (compose
# `sites` profile, nginx, host-networked so the edge can reach it).
# `make sites-list` and `make site-unpublish SLUG=<slug>` manage what is published.
# docs/site-publishing.md.
#
# Where staged sites live on this host. Mounted into the `sites` container.
OLYMPUS_SITES_ROOT=/var/lib/olympus/sites

# --- Published applications (Studio → one container per app) ----------------
# An *application* is full-stack: a React client, a Node API and a SQLite file. It
# is packaged by scripts/package-app.py into an image, and
# scripts/app-runtime.py runs one container per app with its own port and its own
# database. Nothing else about the stack changes — the same wildcard, the same
# `make site-publish SLUG=<slug>` — because the app's name is proxied by a
# generated nginx vhost inside `olympus-sites`, not by a new port at the edge.
#
# Runtime state: `runtime/<slug>.json`, `nginx/<slug>.conf`, and one data directory
# per app holding its SQLite file. Mounted into the `sites` container for the
# vhosts, so it must exist before `make sites-up`.
OLYMPUS_APPS_ROOT=/var/lib/olympus/apps

# The loopback ports apps are given, and how many to try before refusing. Apps bind
# 127.0.0.1 only: `olympus-sites` is host-networked and reaches them, the LAN does
# not, and the only way in is by name through the edge.
APP_PORT_BASE=21400
APP_PORT_RANGE=200
# The port the static server binds, and the port a published site forwards to.
# One variable read by both the server and the publisher, so they cannot drift.
SITE_PORT=20130
# This host's LAN address. The edge is on another machine and reaches back to this
# one, so it must be an address routable from there — same value and same reason as
# GATEWAY_SSO_EDGE_FORWARD_HOST. Leave empty and publishing refuses rather than
# putting a name on the edge that answers nothing.
SITE_EDGE_FORWARD_HOST=
# The suffix a published site answers under: <slug>.<suffix>. The leftmost label is
# the site's directory name, so this suffix and the build slug are the only two
# things that decide where a name points — nothing is typed per publish.
SITE_HOST_SUFFIX=studio.olympus.innotel.us
# A certificate is reused only if it lasts this long; a wildcard inside this window
# is renewed rather than reused. Issuance waits are for `sites-wildcard` only.
SITE_CERT_RENEW_DAYS=30
SITE_CERT_WAIT_SECONDS=180

# --- Cerulean (TrustOps — DNS / NPM hosts / TLS) ----------------------------
# Where the Cerulean control plane answers. The default assumes it is published
# on this same host; when the platform runs elsewhere, point this at it — a
# loopback address that nothing serves silently breaks every DNS/NPM call.
#   platform host: http://<cerulean-host>:3003
CERULEAN_DNS_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3003
# Cerulean's own local login (POST /api/auth/login), NOT the Authentik token and
# NOT the NPM password. `make gateway-edge` uses it to create the DNS record, the
# certificate, and the edge proxy host. It refuses to run on this placeholder.
CERULEAN_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-cerulean-admin
# The zone the public names live in. `make gateway-edge` creates the record inside
# it, and the certificate must be covered by it.
CERULEAN_ZONE=innotel.us

# --- Cerulean Vault (SecretOps — HashiCorp Vault, KV v2) --------------------
# Secrets live in Vault; `.env` carries references, not values:
#   OMNIROUTE_INITIAL_PASSWORD=vault://cerulean/olympus#INITIAL_PASSWORD
# `vault://<mount>/<path>#<key>` is the convention Cerulean resolves at startup,
# so the stack and the platform agree on one format.
#
# On the Cerulean platform Vault already runs as `cerulean-vault` — consume it
# rather than starting another one; never use the root token in
# ./data/vault/init/init.json (0600).
#
# Cerulean's default `cerulean` policy covers <prefix>/data/*, which every
# product sharing the mount can use to read and overwrite the others' secrets.
# Point VAULT_TOKEN_FILE at a token carrying THIS stack's path-scoped `olympus`
# policy instead. The platform's Vault bootstrap mints it — it is the file named
# by VAULT_PRODUCT_TOKENS=olympus over there — and renews it; `make vault-renew`
# renews it from this side too. Copy it over when it is created or re-minted.
#   Then: python3 scripts/vault-bootstrap.py
# For a checkout with no platform Vault, compose.vault.yml provides a dev-mode
# one (in-memory — local iteration only; read the header of that file first).
VAULT_ADDR=
VAULT_TOKEN=
# Preferred over VAULT_TOKEN: a file that both the Vault CLI and this stack read.
VAULT_TOKEN_FILE=./data/vault/token/olympus.token
# KV v2 mount point. Cerulean's default is `cerulean`.
VAULT_PREFIX=cerulean
# Path under the mount where this stack's secrets are stored.
VAULT_PATH=olympus
VAULT_NAMESPACE=
# "1" accepts a self-signed certificate; VAULT_CACERT pins a CA instead.
VAULT_SKIP_VERIFY=
VAULT_CACERT=

# --- Magnate billing (RevenueOps — optional paid seats) ---------------------
MAGNATE_URL=https://magnate.innotel.us
ENTITLEMENTS_API_TOKEN=
# When set, must equal Magnate's ENTITLEMENTS_API_TOKEN.
# Empty = billing APIs are open on a trusted net.

# --- Nginx Proxy Manager (provisioned by Cerulean's own tooling) --------------
# The edge that terminates TLS in front of these hosts. Same caveat as above:
# loopback only works when NPM runs on this host.
#   platform host: http://<npm-host>:81
#
# The admin login for NPM's own API. Cerulean usually holds this and its service
# layer uses it directly, so `make gateway-edge` does NOT need these — they are
# here for reading the edge by hand (GET /api/nginx/proxy-hosts) when a name is
# not answering and Cerulean's view of it needs a second opinion.
NPM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:81
NPM_EMAIL=admin@example.com
NPM_PASSWORD=change-me
